# Every exposed layer lands in exactly one standing. One row: how many layers carry an exposure,
# how many were classified, and how many were classified twice.
#
# The third term sees what the counts cannot
#   A partition fails two ways and the counts only see one of them, so the third term is not
#   decoration. If the JOIN inside layers/exposure_scope drops or fans out a layer, the two
#   cardinalities disagree and that is caught. But the `CASE` sits in the SELECT list, so a layer
#   matching no arm does not vanish: it keeps its row and takes `standing = NULL`. Cardinality is
#   unchanged, and a law built on cardinality is blind to it. `unclassified` is the term that
#   sees it. The general form: every law on this roster is a cardinality identity, and this is
#   not a cardinality property.
#
# The join inside layers/exposure_scope is the risk
#   layers/absorption.sqlc groups the slack matrix by layer, so it is one row per layer by
#   construction. If it ever stops being one, the exposed layers fan out, every standing count
#   still looks reasonable, and the third arm feeds two rules twice.
#
# Which standings exist is the type's job
#   The type is `public.exposure_standing`, because a count of rows per standing cannot see a
#   standing renamed, and `layers/unabsorbed_exposure.sqlc` selects on one by name. Every arm
#   casts to it, so that name is checked when either query is parsed.
#
# Probe
#   examples/probes runs it as `exposure_standing`.
#   Remove the `ELSE` from the `CASE` in layers/exposure_scope.sqlc. The exposed and classified
#   counts still agree and nothing is doubled; `unclassified` must count the layer that falls
#   through, and the law must fail.
-- layers/remainder.sqlc where exposure > 0, against layers/exposure_scope.sqlc.
SELECT a.slug AS law, p.subject, p.holds, p.detail
FROM      (
    :compose(algebra/roster.sqlc)
) a
LEFT JOIN (
    SELECT 'layers/exposure_scope' AS subject,
           x.exposed = x.classified AND x.doubled = 0 AND x.unclassified = 0 AS holds,
           format('%s exposed layers, %s classified, %s classified twice, %s with no standing',
                  x.exposed, x.classified, x.doubled, x.unclassified) AS detail
    FROM ( SELECT
             (SELECT count(*) FROM ( :compose(layers/remainder.sqlc) ) r
               WHERE r.exposure > 1e-9)                                          AS exposed,
             (SELECT count(*) FROM ( :compose(layers/exposure_scope.sqlc) ) z)   AS classified,
             (SELECT count(*) FROM ( SELECT filing, layer
                                     FROM ( :compose(layers/exposure_scope.sqlc) ) z
                                     GROUP BY filing, layer HAVING count(*) > 1 ) d) AS doubled,
             (SELECT count(*) FROM ( :compose(layers/exposure_scope.sqlc) ) z
               WHERE z.standing IS NULL)                                         AS unclassified
         ) x
) p ON true
WHERE a.slug = 'exposure_standing'
